Creating an Ethiopian flag requires using colors and shapes to represent the different elements of the flag. In C++, you can achieve this using a graphics library, such as the Simple DirectMedia Layer (SDL), which allows you to draw graphics onto the screen. Here's sample code to create an Ethiopian flag using the SDL library:

First, you'll need to install the SDL library and set up the development environment accordingly. You can follow the instructions provided on the SDL website: https://www.libsdl.org/download-2.0.php.

Once you have the SDL library set up, use the following code to create the Ethiopian flag:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <SDL.h>

const int WINDOW_WIDTH = 600;
const int WINDOW_HEIGHT = 300;

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (SDL_Init(SDL_INIT_VIDEO) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "SDL could not initialize! SDL_Error: " << SDL_GetError() << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    SDL_Window* window = SDL_CreateWindow("Ethiopian Flag", SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED, SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED, WINDOW_WIDTH, WINDOW_HEIGHT, SDL_WINDOW_SHOWN);
    SDL_Renderer* renderer = SDL_CreateRenderer(window, -1, SDL_RENDERER_ACCELERATED);
    if (!window || !renderer) {
        std::cerr << "Failed to create window or renderer! SDL_Error: " << SDL_GetError() << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    bool quit = false;
    SDL_Event e;
    while (!quit) {
        while (SDL_PollEvent(&e) != 0) {
            if (e.type == SDL_QUIT) {
                quit = true;
            }
        }

        // Clear screen
        SDL_SetRenderDrawColor(renderer, 255, 255, 255, 255);
        SDL_RenderClear(renderer);

        // Green stripe at the top
        SDL_SetRenderDrawColor(renderer, 52, 152, 44, 255);
        SDL_Rect greenStripe = {0, 0, WINDOW_WIDTH, WINDOW_HEIGHT / 3};
        SDL_RenderFillRect(renderer, &greenStripe);

        // Yellow stripe in the middle
        SDL_SetRenderDrawColor(renderer, 249, 177, 20, 255);
        SDL_Rect yellowStripe = {0, WINDOW_HEIGHT / 3, WINDOW_WIDTH, WINDOW_HEIGHT / 3};
        SDL_RenderFillRect(renderer, &yellowStripe);

        // Red stripe at the bottom
        SDL_SetRenderDrawColor(renderer, 213, 30, 36, 255);
        SDL_Rect redStripe = {0, (WINDOW_HEIGHT / 3) * 2, WINDOW_WIDTH, WINDOW_HEIGHT / 3};
        SDL_RenderFillRect(renderer, &redStripe);

        // Update the window
        SDL_RenderPresent(renderer);
    }

    SDL_DestroyRenderer(renderer);
    SDL_DestroyWindow(window);
    SDL_Quit();

    return 0;
}
```

This code will create a window displaying the Ethiopian flag with three horizontal colored stripes: green, yellow, and red.

Keep in mind that the colors, dimensions, and details of the flag can be fine-tuned by adjusting the values in the respective SDL_Rect structures and the SDL_SetRenderDrawColor() calls.

For more details about the SDL library and its functions, please visit the official documentation: https://wiki.libsdl.org/FrontPage

Calibrating a DHT22 sensor typically involves comparing its readings to a reference (like another calibrated sensor or instrument) and then making adjustments to the readings to improve accuracy. Here's an example code for calibrating a DHT22 sensor using an Arduino. You'll need the "DHT.h" library installed, which you can get from the Arduino Library Manager in the Arduino IDE.

```cpp
#include "DHT.h"

#define DHTPIN 2         // Define the pin connected to the DHT22 sensor
#define DHTTYPE DHT22    // Define the sensor type (DHT22)

DHT dht(DHTPIN, DHTTYPE);

// Calibration factors (adjust these values based on your reference sensor or instrument)
float temperatureCalibrationFactor = 1.0;
float humidityCalibrationFactor = 1.0;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600); // Start the serial communication at 9600 baud rate
  dht.begin();        // Initialize the DHT22 sensor
}

void loop() {
  delay(2000); // Wait for 2 seconds to ensure stable sensor readings

  float calTemperature = dht.readTemperature() * temperatureCalibrationFactor;
  float calHumidity = dht.readHumidity() * humidityCalibrationFactor;

  if (isnan(calTemperature) || isnan(calHumidity)) { // Check if any value is NaN
    Serial.println("Failed to read from DHT22 sensor!");
    return;
  }

  Serial.print("Calibrated Temperature: ");
  Serial.print(calTemperature);
  Serial.print("°C ");
  Serial.print("  ");
  Serial.print("Calibrated Humidity: ");
  Serial.print(calHumidity);
  Serial.println("%");
}
```

Replace `temperatureCalibrationFactor` and `humidityCalibrationFactor` variables with the calibration factors based on your reference. These factors can be calculated by comparing the DHT22 sensor's readings to a known reference sensor or instrument.

Upload the code to your Arduino and open the Serial Monitor to observe the calibrated temperature and humidity readings.

