Sure! In Java, you can use the `Pattern` and `Matcher` classes to work with regular expressions. Here's a regex pattern that matches all words where the first five letters are 'Petal' and the last five letters are 'Flower':

```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String regex = "^Petal.*Flower$";
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(regex);

        // Test strings
        String[] testStrings = {
            "PetalFlower",
            "PetalXYZFlower",
            "PetalHelloWorldFlower",
        };

        for (String testString : testStrings) {
            Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(testString);

            if (matcher.find()) {
                System.out.println(testString + " matches the pattern.");
            } else {
                System.out.println(testString + " does not match the pattern.");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This Java code defines a regular expression `^Petal.*Flower$` that will match strings starting with 'Petal', ending with 'Flower', and containing any characters (or none) in between. The `^` and `$` in the regex pattern assert the start and end of the line, respectively, while the `.*` matches zero or more of any character.

