\draw[dashed] (0,2) -- (4,2);
\draw[dashed] (2,0) -- (2,2);

\node[above=of start] {Time = 0};
\node[above=of end] {Time = 1};
\node[left=of start] {(0,0)};
\node[right=of end] {(4,0)};
\node[above=of start] {(0,2)};
\node[above=of end] {(4,2)};
\node[left=of start] {(0,0)};
\node[right=of end] {(4,0)};

\end{tikzpicture}

\begin{align}
v_x &= \dot{x} \\\
v_y &= \dot{y}
\end{align}

\end{document}
```

The diagram shows a person walking from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,0)$. The person's walking speed is given by the vector $v = (v_x, v_y)$, where $v_x$ is the person's horizontal velocity and $v_y$ is the person's vertical velocity.

The mathematical notation in this example is simple, but it can be used to describe more complex situations. For example, if the person is walking on a curved path, the vector $v$ can be used to describe the person's acceleration.

The environmental factors that can affect the person's walking speed and direction include wind, terrain changes, and obstacles. Wind can affect the person's walking speed by pushing the person in one direction or the other. Terrain changes can affect the person's walking speed by making it more difficult or easier for the person to walk. Obstacles can affect the person's walking direction by forcing the person to walk around them.

The following shell command can be used to generate the diagram:

```
dot -Tpng -o person-in-park.png person-in-park.tikz
```

