Sure, I can help you with that! Here's a modified version of the code that takes into account the additional constraints you've mentioned:
```
const weights = [70, 85, 63, 90, 78, 82, 68, 75];
const limit = 1000;

function getCombinations(arr, n) {
  let results = [];
  function combinate(start, prev) {
    if (prev.length === n) {
      results.push(prev);
    } else {
      for (let i = start; i < arr.length; i++) {
        combinate(i + 1, prev.concat(arr[i]));
      }
    }
  }
  combinate(0, []);
  return results;
}

function filterValidCombinations(combinations) {
  let validCombinations = [];
  for (let combination of combinations) {
    if (combination.reduce((acc, curr) => acc + curr, 0) <= limit) {
      let hasOnlyOneHeavyPerson = combination.filter(weight => weight > 85).length <= 1;
      let noPerson1Or2 = !combination.includes(70) || !combination.includes(85);
      if (hasOnlyOneHeavyPerson && noPerson1Or2) {
        validCombinations.push(combination);
      }
    }
  }
  return validCombinations;
}

let possibleCombinations = getCombinations(weights, 8);
let validCombinations = filterValidCombinations(possibleCombinations);
console.log(validCombinations.length);
```
In this modified code, we've added a new function called `filterValidCombinations` that takes the list of all possible combinations and filters out the ones that do not meet the additional constraints.

Inside the `filterValidCombinations` function, we first check if the total weight of the combination is less than or equal to the limit. We then use the `filter` method to find the number of people in the combination who weigh more than 85 kg. We check if this number is less than or equal to 1, which means that there is only one person who weighs more than 85 kg in the combination.

Next, we use the `includes` method to check if the combination includes either person 1 or person 2. If it does not include either of them, we push the combination to the `validCombinations` array.

Finally, we return the `validCombinations` array, which contains all the combinations that meet all the constraints.

Note that the `getCombinations` function remains unchanged, as it already takes care of the basic constraint of selecting 8 people from the list of weights. The additional constraints are applied in the `filterValidCombinations` function.

