Regarding your question on using Axios with Windows system proxy settings, I must inform you that Axios is designed to work without relying on the operating system's proxy settings. However, you may still be able to configure Axios to use the system proxy settings if you wish.
To do this, you can use the `proxy` option when creating the Axios instance. Here's an example:
```typescript
import axios from 'axios';
const axiosInstance = axios.create({
  proxy: {
    // Set the system proxy settings
    // 'http://proxy.example.com:8080' for a HTTP proxy,
    // 'localhost:8080' for a HTTPS proxy,
  }
});
```
By providing the `proxy` option, you can configure Axios to use the system proxy settings, which may be more reliable and faster than using the `https://proxy.example.com` syntax.
Please note that this is a legacy feature, and it's recommended to use the `https://proxy.example.com` syntax instead, as it's more flexible and can handle different types of proxies.
