There are a few ways to pass data from one view to another in a single-page application.

One way is to use query parameters. Query parameters are added to the URL of a page, and they can be used to pass data to the page. For example, if you want to pass the name of a user to a page, you could add the query parameter ?username=JohnDoe to the URL of the page.

Another way to pass data from one view to another is to use local storage. Local storage is a way to store data in the browser. To use local storage, you need to create a variable and then assign it a value. For example, you could create a variable called `username` and assign it the value `JohnDoe`. You can then access the data in local storage by using the `getItem()` and `setItem()` methods.

Finally, you can also pass data from one view to another using the `this.props` object. The `props` object is a property of all React components, and it contains all of the data that is passed to the component. To pass data to another view, you need to add it to the `props` object of the component that you want to pass it to. For example, if you have a component called `Home` and you want to pass the name of a user to it, you could add the user name to the `props` object of `Home` like this:

```
const Home = () => {
  const { username } = this.props;
  return <h1>Welcome, {username}</h1>;
}
```

When you render `Home`, the `username` property will be passed to it, and you can access it in the component like this:

```
<h1>Welcome, {this.props.username}</h1>;
```

The best way to pass data from one view to another depends on the specific needs of your application. If you only need to pass a small amount of data, query parameters are a good option. If you need to pass more data, local storage or `this.props` are better options.

