To write a PowerShell script to check folders and their ACLs for SIDs that only occur in the SIDHistory of users or groups in the domain, you can use the following code:

```powershell
# Import the required module for Active Directory
Import-Module ActiveDirectory

# Define the starting folder 
$startingFolder = "C:\path\to\folder"

# Define a function for checking SIDHistory
function Check-SIDHistory ($sid) {
    # Get all user objects in the domain
    $users = Get-ADUser -Filter * -Properties SIDHistory
    foreach ($user in $users) {
        if ($user.SIDHistory -contains $sid) {
            return $true
        }
    }

    # Get all group objects in the domain
    $groups = Get-ADGroup -Filter * -Properties SIDHistory
    foreach ($group in $groups) {
        if ($group.SIDHistory -contains $sid) {
            return $true
        }
    }

    return $false
}

# Define a function for checking folder ACLs
function Check-FolderACLs ($folderPath) {
    $folderACLs = Get-Acl -Path $folderPath
    foreach ($accessRule in $folderACLs.Access) {
        $sid = $accessRule.IdentityReference.Value
        if (Check-SIDHistory $sid) {
            Write-Host "SID $sid found in SIDHistory for folder $folderPath"
        }
    }
}

# Check the starting folder
Check-FolderACLs $startingFolder

# Recursively check sub-folders
Get-ChildItem -Path $startingFolder -Directory -Recurse | ForEach-Object {
    Check-FolderACLs $_.FullName
}
```
Note: Replace "C:\path\to\folder" with the path of the folder you want to start from. This script will check the starting folder and all its sub-folders. Make sure you run this script as a user with appropriate permissions to read Active Directory objects and folder ACLs.

This script does the following:
- Imports the ActiveDirectory module to fetch users and groups.
- Defines a function `Check-SIDHistory` to check if a SID exists in the SIDHistory of any user or group.
- Defines a function `Check-FolderACLs` to check folder ACLs for SIDs that only occur in the SIDHistory.
- Calls `Check-FolderACLs` for the starting folder.
- Recursively checks the subfolders using `Get-ChildItem`.

