In it, you can see that the modulo and division operations are being done on 64-bit values. Can this function be revised so that module and division are done on 32-bit values?

output:
Yes, you can definitely revise the function to perform modulo and division operations on 32-bit values. To do this, change the input type from `uint64_t` to `uint32_t` and adjust the calculations accordingly. Note that the maximum value for `uint32_t` is 4,294,967,295, so make sure your input values don't exceed this limit. Here's the revised version of your function:

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define MS_IN_HOUR 3600000U
#define MS_IN_MINUTE 60000U
#define MS_IN_SECOND 1000U

void vk_util_format_timestamp(uint32_t timestamp, char buffer[13])
{
    if (buffer == NULL) {
        return;
    }

    uint8_t hours = (timestamp / MS_IN_HOUR) % 24;
    uint8_t minutes = (timestamp % MS_IN_HOUR) / MS_IN_MINUTE;
    uint8_t seconds = (timestamp % MS_IN_MINUTE) / MS_IN_SECOND;
    uint16_t milliseconds = timestamp % MS_IN_SECOND;

    snprintf(buffer, 13, "%02d:%02d:%02d.%03d",
             hours, minutes, seconds, milliseconds);
}
```

This revised function will now perform modulo and division operations on 32-bit values while giving you the same output format as before. Keep in mind that the timestamp input should now be within the range of `uint32_t`.

