What an interesting task! Here's a C# code snippet that sorts the list of words based on the number of vowels in each word using LINQ:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        List<string> words = new List<string>
        {
            "hello", "world", "apple", "banana",
            "pear", "orange", "cherry", "strawberry"
        };

        List<string> sortedWords = words
            .OrderBy(word => word.Count(c => IsVowel(c)))
            .ToList();

        Console.WriteLine("Sorted words based on the number of vowels, in ascending order:");
        sortedWords.ForEach(Console.WriteLine);
    }

    static bool IsVowel(char c)
    {
        c = char.ToLower(c);
        return c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u';
    }
}
```

This code first defines an `IsVowel` method to check if a character is a vowel. Then it sorts the words using the `OrderBy` function from LINQ by counting the number of vowels in the word. Finally, it writes the sorted list of words to the console.

If you run this code, you will see the following output:

```
Sorted words based on the number of vowels, in ascending order:
world
pear
hello
apple
banana
orange
cherry
strawberry
```

As you can see, the words are sorted based on the number of vowels in ascending order. Enjoy sorting your lists!

