We then calculate the correlation coefficient between `FurColor` and `SunbathingPreference` using the `corrcoef` function. We also calculate the p-value associated with the correlation coefficient.

Next, we perform a two-sample t-test using the `ttest` function to determine if there is a significant difference in sunbathing preference between cats with light and dark fur colors. We calculate the p-value associated with the t-test.

Finally, we display the results using `fprintf`. The `corrcoef` function returns a matrix with the correlation coefficient and p-value, so we extract the p-value from the matrix using indexing (`p-value = stats(2,1)`). The `ttest` function returns a structure with the test statistic and p-value, so we extract the p-value from the structure using dot notation (`p-value = stats.p_value`).

Note that this code assumes that the `cat_data.csv` file contains the following columns: `FurColor` (string, either 'Light' or 'Dark') and `SunbathingPreference` ( binary, either 0 or 1). You can modify the code accordingly if your dataset has different column names or data types.

