Java code:

import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ReviewParser {

public static void main(String[] args) {
    String filename = "reviews.txt";
    String newLine = "";
    List<String> reviews = new ArrayList<>();
    try {
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(filename));
        // Get the first line (header)
        String firstLine = reader.readLine();
        String[] header = firstLine.split(",");
        reviews= new ArrayList<>();
        reviews.add(header[0]);
        reviews.add(header[1]);
        reviews.add(header[2]);
        reviews.add(header[3]);
        reviews.add(header[4]);
        reviews.add(header[5]);
        reviews.add(header[6]);
        reviews.add(header[7]);
        while ((newLine = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            // Find user feedback and pricing data
            int userFeedbackIndex = newLine.indexOf("User Feedback:");
            int pricingDataIndex = newLine.indexOf("Price:");
            // Extract user feedback and pricing data
            int userFeedbackEndIndex = newLine.indexOf("\n", userFeedbackIndex);
            String userFeedback = newLine.substring(userFeedbackIndex, userFeedbackEndIndex);
            int pricingDataEndIndex = newLine.indexOf("\n", pricingDataIndex);
            String pricingData = newLine.substring(pricingDataIndex, pricingDataEndIndex);
            // Add the reviewed product information including user feedback and pricing data
            reviews.add(userFeedback + "," + pricingData);
        }
    } catch (IOException e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
    try {
        FileWriter writer = new FileWriter(filename + "_modified");
        for (String review : reviews) {
            writer.write(review);
        }
    } catch (IOException e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}

}

To retrieve and analyze peer-reviewed academic journal articles related to archaeological anthropology using MATLAB code, you can follow these steps:

1. Gathering Data: To find relevant articles, you can use APIs provided by various scholarly databases like CrossRef, Scopus, Web of Science, or Europe PMC. These databases often have APIs that allow you to fetch scholarly articles based on specific search queries related to the topic of archaeological anthropology.

Example: Using the CrossRef API.
The base URL of the API is `https://api.crossref.org/works?query=`, followed by the search query and other filters.

2. Define your query: Define search terms and filters that represent your topic of interest. Example: "archaeological anthropology".

3. Use the `webread` function in MATLAB to fetch data from the scholarly database API. You may need to specify the API key as a parameter if needed. `webread` returns the output in JSON format by default.

```MATLAB
query = 'archaeological+anthropology';
api_key = 'your_api_key'; % if required by the database
url = ['https://api.crossref.org/works?query=', query]; % Add other filters if needed
options = weboptions('Timeout', 60);
data = webread(url, options);
```

4. Parse the JSON data to extract relevant information like the journal title, authors, publication date, keywords, etc.

```MATLAB
% Convert JSON data to MATLAB structure
articles = jsondecode(data);

% Extract relevant information for each article
num_articles = length(articles.message.items);
parsed_data = cell(num_articles, 5);

for i = 1:num_articles
    parsed_data{i, 1} = articles.message.items(i).title{1};
    parsed_data{i, 2} = articles.message.items(i).container-title{1};

    % Extract author names
    authors = articles.message.items(i).author;
    authors_list = '';
    for j = 1:length(authors)
        authors_list = [authors_list, authors(j).given, ' ', authors(j).family];
        if j ~= length(authors)
            authors_list = [authors_list, ', '];
        end
    end
    parsed_data{i, 3} = authors_list;

    parsed_data{i, 4} = articles.message.items(i).published.print.date_parts{1}(1);
    parsed_data{i, 5} = articles.message.items(i).URL;
end
```

5. Analyze the parsed data by using MATLAB's built-in data analysis and visualization functions. You can look for trends, perform textual analysis, create graphs, and visualize patterns within the data.

Example: Year-wise publication count
```MATLAB
years = cell2mat(parsed_data(:, 4));
[counts, bins] = hist(years, unique(years));
bar(bins, counts);
xlabel('Year');
ylabel('Publication count');
title('Archaeological Anthropology Publications by Year');
```

Remember that each scholarly database has its specific API requirements, and query options might vary. You should check their documentation for specific details.

By following the above process, you can automate retrieving and analyzing academic journal articles related to archaeological anthropology in MATLAB.

