Sure, here is a sample Node.js code that receives a web hook notification from SharePoint where a new file is created there, receives metadata about the new file and then copies that new file into an Azure blob container, applying a retention policy based on the file metadata:


const { AzureFunction, Context, HttpRequest } = require('azure-functions');
const { BlobClient, ContainerClient } = require('@azure/storage-blob');

const functionName = 'copy-file-to-blob-container';
const storageAccountName = 'mystorageaccountname';
const storageAccountKey = 'mystorageaccountkey';
const blobContainerName = 'myblobcontainername';
const retentionPolicyName = 'myretentionpolicyname';

const trigger = async function (context: Context, req: HttpRequest) {
  // Get the file metadata from the request
  const fileMetadata = req.body.fileMetadata;

  // Create a blob client for the target container
  const blobClient = new BlobClient(
    `https://${storageAccountName}.blob.core.windows.net/${blobContainerName}/${fileMetadata.name}`,
    storageAccountKey
  );

  // Copy the file to the blob container
  await blobClient.copyFromLocalFile(fileMetadata.localPath);

  // Apply the retention policy to the blob
  const retentionPolicy = await blobClient.getRetentionPolicy();
  await retentionPolicy.set(retentionPolicyName);

  // Return the response
  context.res = {
    status: 200,
    body: {
      message: `File ${fileMetadata.name} has been copied to the blob container and a retention policy has been applied.`
    }
  };
};

const run = async (context: Context, req: HttpRequest) => {
  try {
    await trigger(context, req);
  } catch (error) {
    context.res = {
      status: 500,
      body: {
        message: `Error copying file: ${error}`
      }
    };
  }
};

exports.run = run;

You can create a JavaScript hook for `console.log` by overriding the default behavior and implementing your own custom logic. Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```javascript
// Save the original console.log function
const originalConsoleLog = console.log;

// Create a custom function that will act as a hook
function customConsoleLog(...args) {
  // Process the arguments or add any custom behavior
  // Example: Print a timestamp
  const timestamp = new Date().toLocaleString();
  originalConsoleLog(timestamp + ' | ', ...args);

  // Call the original console.log function with the modified arguments
  // You can also add any other logic here, if needed
}

// Override the default console.log function with the custom function
console.log = customConsoleLog;

// Now, when calling console.log, it will use the custom function
console.log('Hello, World!');
```

This code snippet saves the original `console.log` function, defines a custom hook, and overrides the default behavior to include a timestamp for every log message. Modify the custom function as needed to fit your requirements.

To explain the role of white blood cells (WBCs) in the immune system, I'll use an example in C# involving two common types of white blood cells: Neutrophils and Lymphocytes, as well as a cytokine named interleukin-1 (IL-1) involved in the immune response:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public abstract class WhiteBloodCell
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public abstract void Defend();

    public void ReleaseCytokine(Cytokine cytokine)
    {
        cytokine.Trigger();
    }
}

public class Neutrophil : WhiteBloodCell
{
    public Neutrophil()
    {
       Name = "Neutrophil";
    }

    public override void Defend()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"The {Name} attacks and destroys bacteria directly.");
    }
}

public class Lymphocyte : WhiteBloodCell
{
    public Lymphocyte()
    {
        Name = "Lymphocyte";
    }

    public override void Defend()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"The {Name} produces antibodies and coordinates the immune response.");
    }
}

public class Cytokine
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public Cytokine(string name)
    {
        Name = name;
    }

    public void Trigger()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Name} gets released and helps in immune cell signaling and activation.");
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        List<WhiteBloodCell> wbcList = new() 
        {
            new Neutrophil(),
            new Lymphocyte()
        };

        Cytokine il1 = new Cytokine("Interleukin-1");

        foreach (WhiteBloodCell wbc in wbcList)
        {
            wbc.Defend();
            wbc.ReleaseCytokine(il1);
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, `WhiteBloodCell` is an abstract class representing the overall functionality of white blood cells. Then, the `Neutrophil` and `Lymphocyte` classes inherit from the `WhiteBloodCell` class and each provides its specific defense mechanism in the `Defend()` method.

The `Cytokine` class represents cytokines in the immune response, and here we have used Interleukin-1 (IL-1) as an example. The `ReleaseCytokine()` method signifies the role cytokines play in aiding the white blood cells during an immune response.

In the `Main` method, we have created instances of both white blood cell types and the cytokine, and then demonstrated their defense process, as well as cytokine release through the loop.

This code demonstrates a simplified explanation of how Neutrophils, Lymphocytes, and a cytokine like IL-1 interact with each other in the immune response, using a C# code representation.

