First of all, it's important to clarify that scanning for Bluetooth devices in the background, while the app is idle, is discouraged and has limitations on both Android and iOS. This can lead to privacy concerns and battery drain.

However, I can provide a simple example function using the `react-native-ble-plx` library that scans for Bluetooth devices while the app is active. You can later adjust this function to meet the exact behavior you require.

To create a React Native app that can scan Bluetooth devices, follow these steps:

1. Install the `react-native-ble-plx` library:

```bash
npm install --save react-native-ble-plx
```

2. Link the library (only needed if you're using React Native <= 0.59):

```bash
react-native link react-native-ble-plx
```

3. Here is a minimal React Native component that scans and logs Bluetooth devices:

```javascript
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';
import { View, Text } from 'react-native';
import { BleManager } from 'react-native-ble-plx';

const BluetoothScanner = () => {
  const manager = new BleManager();

  useEffect(() => {
    const startScanning = async () => {
      const permission = await manager.state();

      if (permission !== 'PoweredOn') {
        console.log('Please turn on Bluetooth.');
        return;
      }

      manager.startDeviceScan(null, null, (error, device) => {
        if (error) {
          console.log('Scanning error:', error);
          return;
        }

        if (device) {
          console.log('Device found:', device.name, device.id);
        }
      });

      // Stop scanning after 10 seconds
      setTimeout(() => {
        manager.stopDeviceScan();
      }, 10000);
    };

    startScanning();

    return () => {
      manager.stopDeviceScan();
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <View style={{ flex: 1, justifyContent: 'center', alignItems: 'center' }}>
      <Text>Scanning for Bluetooth Devices...</Text>
    </View>
  );
};

export default BluetoothScanner;
```

Place this component within your app, and it will start scanning for Bluetooth devices when it mounts. The list of discovered devices will be logged in your console. Remember that this code will only work while the app is active.

For more advanced use cases, please refer to the `react-native-ble-plx` library documentation: https://github.com/Polidea/react-native-ble-plx

