Unfortunately, there isn't a direct method to achieve this using Google Apps Script. When a script runs in a shared Google Sheet, it runs in the context of the user currently using the Sheet. This means that the Gmail API and other user-specific APIs will run with the current user's permissions, and not the owner's.

However, a possible workaround is to use a Google Cloud Platform (GCP) project with a service account.

1. Create a GCP project and enable the Gmail API: https://console.cloud.google.com/
2. Create a service account with domain-wide delegation: https://console.cloud.google.com/iam-admin/
3. Grant the service account necessary permissions in the Google Admin Console: https://admin.google.com/
4. Use the service account's credentials file within the script to impersonate the owner.
5. Adjust your code to use the service account to access the Gmail API.

Here's an example of how you can use a service account in Google Apps Script to access the Gmail API:

1. Import the `googleapis` library in your Google Apps Script project following this guide: https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/libraries#writing_the_scripts
2. Set up your script to use the service account's JSON key file (shared as a URL or stored in Google Sheets as a base64-encoded string).

```javascript
const PRIVATE_KEY = '-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----....'; // Your private key
const CLIENT_EMAIL = 'xxxx@YOUR_PROJECT_ID.iam.gserviceaccount.com'; // Your service account email
const USER_EMAIL = 'owneremail@gmail.com'; // The owner's email

function getService() {
  return OAuth2.createService('Gmail:' + USER_EMAIL)
    .setTokenUrl('https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/token')
    .setPrivateKey(PRIVATE_KEY)
    .setClientId(CLIENT_EMAIL)
    .setIssuer(CLIENT_EMAIL)
    .setPropertyStore(PropertiesService.getScriptProperties())
    .setParam('subject_token_type', 'urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt')
    .setScope(['https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly'])
    .setSubject(USER_EMAIL);
}

function getDataFromGmailAPI() {
  const service = getService();

  if (!service.hasAccess()) {
    Logger.log('Error: %s', service.getLastError());
    return;
  }

  const url = 'https://www.googleapis.com/gmail/v1/users/me/messages';
  const response = UrlFetchApp.fetch(url, {
    headers: {
      'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + service.getAccessToken()
    }
  });

  const data = JSON.parse(response.getContentText());
  Logger.log(data);
}
```

Now, anyone clicking the button would be accessing the Gmail API using the owner's context as long as the service account impersonates the owner.

Remember to keep the service account key file secure, as it grants access to the owner's Gmail API.

