Sure. The following code shows an example of how to refactor a call to the deprecated `java.util.logging.LogRecord.setMillis` method:

```
import java.util.logging.LogRecord;

public class MyClass {

    public void doSomething() {
        LogRecord record = new LogRecord(Level.INFO, "Some message");
        record.setMillis(System.currentTimeMillis());
        logger.log(record);
    }
}
```

To refactor this code, we can use the `java.time` package to create a new `Instant` object that represents the current time. We can then pass this `Instant` object to the `LogRecord.setInstant` method instead of the deprecated `setMillis` method. The following code shows how to do this:

```
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.logging.LogRecord;

public class MyClass {

    public void doSomething() {
        LogRecord record = new LogRecord(Level.INFO, "Some message");
        record.setInstant(Instant.now());
        logger.log(record);
    }
}
```

This code is now using the `java.time` package instead of the deprecated `java.util.logging` package. This means that it will continue to work even after the `java.util.logging` package is eventually deprecated.

