```R
# First, let's create some sample data for sandwiches and restrictions data frames
sandwiches <- data.frame(
  customer = c("Alice", "Bob", "Carol", "Dave", "Eva"),
  ingredients = c("ham, cheese, lettuce", "turkey, avocado, tomato", "egg, spinach, mayo", "peanut butter, jelly", "chicken, mayo, pickles")
)

restrictions <- data.frame(
  customer = c("Alice", "Bob", "Carol", "George"),
  restriction = c("lactose intolerant", "vegetarian", "vegan", "nut allergy")
)

# Merge data frames based on the `customer` column
merged_data <- merge(sandwiches, restrictions, by = "customer", all = TRUE)

# Define a function to check if a customer's sandwich fits their restrictions
check_restrictions <- function(ingredient_list, restriction_type) {
  # Modify the checks based on the exact dietary restrictions needed
  ingredient_list <- tolower(ingredient_list)

  if (restriction_type == "lactose intolerant") {
    return(!grepl("cheese|mayo", ingredient_list))
  } else if(restriction_type == "vegetarian") {
    return(!grepl("ham|turkey|chicken", ingredient_list))
  } else if(restriction_type == "vegan") {
    return(!grepl("ham|turkey|chicken|egg|cheese|mayo", ingredient_list))
  } else if(restriction_type == "nut allergy") {
    return(!grepl("peanut", ingredient_list))
  } else {
    return(TRUE)
  }
}

# Check if each customer's sandwich fits their restrictions and store the result in a new column
merged_data$restriction_met <- mapply(check_restrictions, merged_data$ingredients, merged_data$restriction)

# Generate a list of customers with their dietary restrictions and whether their sandwich meets the restriction
customers_with_restrictions <- merged_data[!is.na(merged_data$restriction), c("customer", "restriction", "restriction_met")]

# Display the resulting data frame
customers_with_restrictions
```

The above R code creates two sample data frames (sandwiches and restrictions) and merges them based on the customer column (merged_data). A custom function "check_restrictions()" is defined to check for specific dietary restrictions based on sandwich ingredients. It then uses the merged data along with the "mapply()" function to populate a new "restriction_met" column with TRUE or FALSE values, depending on whether the restriction is met. Finally, it generates a data frame with a list of customers, their dietary restrictions, and a boolean column indicating if the sandwich meets the restriction. Note that the sample ingredients and restrictions used in this code snippet may need to be modified depending on the actual set of dietary restrictions needed.

Here's a simple JavaScript program that creates pop-up notifications with customizable content and style. This program relies on HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

First, prepare the HTML file:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Pop-up Notifications</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div id="notification-container"></div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

Then, create the CSS file (styles.css) to style the notifications:
```css
.notification {
    position: absolute;
    padding: 10px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    z-index: 1000;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.3s;
}

.visible {
    opacity: 1;
}
```

Lastly, implement the JavaScript functionality (script.js). This script will handle the custom notification options and error handling:
```javascript
function showNotification(options) {
    const {
        text = "This is a notification",
        duration = 3000,
        position = "top-right",
        backgroundColor = "blue",
        textColor = "white",
        fontFamily = "Arial",
    } = options;

    const notificationContainer = document.getElementById("notification-container");
    const notification = document.createElement("div");

    notification.classList.add("notification");
    notification.style.backgroundColor = backgroundColor;
    notification.style.color = textColor;
    notification.style.fontFamily = fontFamily;

    switch (position) {
        case "top-right":
            notification.style.top = "10px";
            notification.style.right = "10px";
            break;
        case "top-left":
            notification.style.top = "10px";
            notification.style.left = "10px";
            break;
        case "bottom-right":
            notification.style.bottom = "10px";
            notification.style.right = "10px";
            break;
        case "bottom-left":
            notification.style.bottom = "10px";
            notification.style.left = "10px";
            break;
        default:
            console.error("Invalid position. Please choose: top-left, top-right, bottom-left, or bottom-right.");
            return;
    }

    notification.innerText = text;
    notificationContainer.append(notification);

    setTimeout(() => {
        notification.classList.add("visible");
    }, 50);

    setTimeout(() => {
        notification.classList.remove("visible");

        setTimeout(() => {
            notification.remove();
        }, 300);
    }, duration);
}

showNotification({
    text: "This is a custom notification!",
    duration: 5000,
    position: "bottom-left",
    backgroundColor: "green",
    textColor: "white",
    fontFamily: "Calibri",
});
```

This script defines the `showNotification` function, which accepts an options object with values for text, duration, position, backgroundColor, textColor, and fontFamily. It also includes error handling for invalid position values. To customize the notification, simply modify the options passed to the `showNotification` function.

A palindrome is a word, phrase, number, or other sequence of characters which reads the same backward as forward, such as madam or racecar.

To check if a given array x[0..n-1] is a palindrome, we can use the following algorithm:

1. Initialize two pointers, i and j, to 0 and n - 1, respectively.
2. While i < j:
    If x[i] != x[j]:
        return false;
    i = i + 1;
    j = j - 1;
3. Return true;

Here is a Python implementation of the algorithm:

```def is_palindrome(x):
    if len(x) <= 1:
        return True
    i = 0
    j = len(x) - 1
    while i < j:
        if x[i] != x[j]:
            return False
        i = i + 1
        j = j - 1
    return True
```

Here is a Java implementation of the algorithm:

```public boolean isPalindrome(String x) {
    if (x.length() <= 1) {
        return true;
    }
    int i = 0;
    int j = x.length() - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        if (x.charAt(i) != x.charAt(j)) {
            return false;
        }
        i++;
        j--;
    }
    return true;
}
```

Here is a C++ implementation of the algorithm:

```bool isPalindrome(const std::string& x) {
    if (x.length() <= 1) {
        return true;
    }
    int i = 0;
    int j = x.length() - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        if (x[i] != x[j]) {
            return false;
        }
        i++;
        j--;
    }
    return true;
}
```

Here is a C implementation of the algorithm:

```bool isPalindrome(char *x) {
    if (strlen(x) <= 1) {
        return true;
    }
    int i = 0;
    int j = strlen(x) - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        if (x[i] != x[j]) {
            return false;
        }
        i++;
        j--;
    }
    return true;
}
```

