To create a schema for your course upload form in Sanity headless CMS, you need to follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have the Sanity CLI installed. If not, you can install it by running `npm install -g @sanity/cli`.

2. Create a new Sanity project by typing `sanity init` in the terminal and following the on-screen prompts. Choose the blank schema template for a clean starting point.

3. Inside the `schemas` folder, create a new file named `course.js` that will contain the schema for your course document.

4. Modify `course.js` and add the following schema definition:

```javascript
import { MdLocalMovies as icon } from 'react-icons/md';

export default {
  title: 'Course',
  name: 'course',
  type: 'document',
  icon,
  fields: [
    {
      title: 'Title',
      name: 'title',
      type: 'string',
    },
    {
      title: 'Description',
      name: 'description',
      type: 'text',
    },
    {
      title: 'Chapters',
      name: 'chapters',
      type: 'array',
      of: [
        {
          type: 'object',
          name: 'chapter',
          title: 'Chapter',
          fields: [
            {
              title: 'Lesson',
              name: 'lessons',
              type: 'array',
              of: [
                {
                  title: 'Name',
                  name: 'name',
                  type: 'string',
                },
                {
                  title: 'Video',
                  name: 'video',
                  type: 'file',
                  options: {
                    accept: 'video/*',
                  },
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
```

This schema for 'Course' defines the `title`, `description`, and `chapters` fields. The `chapters` field contains an array of chapter objects, and each chapter has a `lessons` array containing the `name` and `video` fields for each lesson.

5. Next, you need to include this schema in your `schema.js` file. In the `schemas` folder, open the `schema.js` file and import the `course` schema you've just created, and add it to the `types` array:

```javascript
import createSchema from 'part:@sanity/base/schema-creator';
import schemaTypes from 'all:part:@sanity/base/schema-type';

// Import your course schema
import course from './course';

export default createSchema({
  name: 'default',
  types: schemaTypes.concat([
    /* Your custom types */
    course,
  ]),
});
```

6. Start your Sanity Studio by running `sanity start`, and you'll now have a schema form for uploading courses with your specified fields.

7. To deploy your Studio and make any front-end connection to your Sanity project, you can run `sanity deploy`.

Now, you can use this schema form for uploading courses with the title and description fields, as well as the nested structure for chapters and lessons with their respective names and videos.

Sure, here is a method in C# that saves the information about which horse is faster from the start than other horses:

```c#
public static void SaveHorseInformation(List<RaceParticipant> raceParticipants)
{
  // Create a dictionary to store the information about the horses.
  Dictionary<int, Horse> horses = new Dictionary<int, Horse>();

  // Iterate through the race participants and add them to the dictionary.
  foreach (RaceParticipant raceParticipant in raceParticipants)
  {
    int horseId = raceParticipant.HorseId;
    horses[horseId] = new Horse
    {
      Id = horseId,
      HorseName = raceParticipant.HorseName,
      RiderName = raceParticipant.RiderName,
      Earnings = raceParticipant.Earnings,
      VoltTime = raceParticipant.VoltTime,
      AutoTime = raceParticipant.AutoTime,
    };
  }

  // Find the fastest horse.
  Horse fastestHorse = null;
  for (int i = 0; i < horses.Count; i++)
  {
    Horse horse = horses[i];
    if (fastestHorse == null || horse.AutoTime < fastestHorse.AutoTime)
    {
      fastestHorse = horse;
    }
  }

  // Save the information about the fastest horse.
  fastestHorse.IsFastest = true;

  // Save the information about the other horses.
  foreach (Horse horse in horses)
  {
    if (horse != fastestHorse)
    {
      horse.IsFastest = false;
    }
  }

  // Save the information about the horses to a file.
  File.WriteAllText("horses.txt", JsonConvert.SerializeObject(horses));
}

public class Horse
{
  public int Id { get; set; }
  public string HorseName { get; set; }
  public string RiderName { get; set; }
  public int Earnings { get; set; }
  public string VoltTime { get; set; }
  public string AutoTime { get; set; }
  public bool IsFastest { get; set; }
}
```

This method takes a list of race participants as input and saves the information about the fastest horse to a file. The method first creates a dictionary to store the information about the horses. It then iterates through the race participants and adds them to the dictionary. The method then finds the fastest horse and saves the information about the fastest horse to the dictionary. The method then saves the information about the other horses to the dictionary. Finally, the method saves the information about the horses to a file.

To remove the default header from a React Navigation screen, you can use the `headerLeft` and `headerRight` props on the `NavigationScreenOptions` object. For example, the following code would remove the default header from the `SplashScreen` screen:

```
import React from 'react';
import { NavigationScreenOptions } from 'react-navigation';

const SplashScreen = ({ navigation }) => {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Splash Screen</Text>
    </View>
  );
};

