To accomplish this, you'll need to use a web service like Alamofire or URLSession to make a request to the API and retrieve the data. Once you have the data, you can use Swift's built-in JSON parsing functions to parse the data and display it to the user in a table or list view.

Here's an example of how you can use Alamofire to retrieve data from an API and display it to the user:
```swift
import UIKit
import Alamofire

class ViewController: UIViewController {

    // Define the URL of the API
    let apiEndpoint = "https://example.com/api/recommendations"

    // Define the parameters to pass to the API (e.g. cuisine type, price range, location)
    let parameters = ["cuisine": "italian", "price_range": "moderate", "location": "New York"]

    // Use Alamofire to make a request to the API
    func retrieveRecommendations() {
        Alamofire.request(apiEndpoint, parameters: parameters).responseJSON { response in

            // Check if the request was successful
            if response.result.isSuccess {

                // Parse the data from the API response
                let data = response.result.value as! [Any]

                // Display the data to the user in a table view
                let tableView = self.view.Subview(withId: "tableView") as! UITableView
                tableView.delegate = self
                tableView.dataSource = self
                tableView.reloadData()
            } else {

                // Display an error message to the user
                let errorMessage = "Error retrieving recommendations: \(response.result.error?.localizedDescription?? "Unknown error")"
                let errorAlert = UIAlertController(title: "Error", message: errorMessage, preferredStyle:.alert)
                let okAction = UIAlertAction(title: "OK", style:.default) { _ in
                    // Handle the error
                }
                errorAlert.addAction(okAction)
                self.present(errorAlert, animated: true, completion: nil)
            }
        }
    }

    // Define the table view delegate and data source methods
    func numberOfSections(in tableView: UITableView) -> Int {
        return 1
    }

    func tableView(_ tableView: UITableView, numberOfRowsInSection section: Int) -> Int {
        return data.count
    }

    func tableView(_ tableView: UITableView, cellForRowAt indexPath: IndexPath) -> UITableViewCell {
        let cell = tableView.dequeueReusableCell(withIdentifier: "recommendationCell", for: indexPath)

        // Configure the cell with the data
        let recommendation = data[indexPath.row]
        cell.textLabel?.text = recommendation["name"] as? String
        cell.detailTextLabel?.text = recommendation["price"] as? String

        return cell
    }

    // Define any other view controller methods you need
}
```
In this example, we define the API endpoint and the parameters to pass to the API, then use Alamofire to make a request to the API. Once we have the data, we parse it and display it to the user in a table view.

Keep in mind that this is just an example and you'll need to modify it to fit your specific needs. Additionally, you'll need to ensure that the API is properly authenticated and authorized, and that the data is properly encrypted and secure.

