```matlab
% Define the numbers to be used
nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9];

% Create a 3x3 grid
grid = zeros(3, 3);
for i = 1:3
    for j = 1:3
        grid(i, j) = nums(i + j - 1);
    end
end

% Calculate the sum of each row, column, and diagonal
row_sums = sum(grid, 1);
col_sums = sum(grid, 2);
diag_sums = sum(grid, [1 2; 2 3]);

% Plot the grid and the sums
figure;
plot(grid);
title('9-Point Circle Puzzle: Solution');
xlabel('Position in Grid');
ylabel('Value of Number');

hold on;
plot(row_sums, '--`, `red', 'Linewidth', 2);
plot(col_sums, ``, `green`, `Linewidth`, 2);
plot(diag_sums, ``, `blue`, `Linewidth`, 2);

legend('Row Sums', 'Column Sums', 'Diagonal Sums');
```
Now, let me explain how this visualization helped me overcome the challenge of solving this puzzle. Firstly, by using the visualization, I could easily see the relationship between the numbers in the grid and the sums of each row, column, and diagonal. This helped me identify the pattern in the puzzle and find the correct solution.

Furthermore, the visualization also helped me to quickly identify any errors in my thinking and correct them. For example, if I had made a mistake in my calculation, I could have easily spotted it by looking at the plot and identifying the incorrect sum.

