To extract a specific certificate from a certificate bundle in bash, you can use tools like `awk` and `grep`. Here's a script that extracts a certificate whose issuer matches a given string:

```bash
#!/bin/bash

# Replace the searchString with the desired issuer string
searchString="Issuer String"

# Replace the bundleFilePath with the path to your cert bundle file
bundleFilePath="path/to/your/cert-bundle.crt"

awk -v search="$searchString" '
  BEGIN { cert = 0; output_block = 0; }

  /-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----/ {
    block = 1;
    buffer = $0RS;
    next;
  }

  /-----END CERTIFICATE-----/ { block = 0; buffer = buffer $0; }

  block { buffer = buffer $0RS; next; }

  /Issuer:/ {
    if (match($0, search)) {
      output_block = 1;
    }
  }

  /^$/ {
    if (output_block) {
      printf "%s", buffer;
      output_block = 0;
    }
  }
' "$bundleFilePath"
```

Replace `"Issuer String"` with the issuer identifier you are looking for in the certificate. Also, replace `"path/to/your/cert-bundle.crt"` with the path to your certificate bundle file.

Save this script to a file, make it executable with `chmod +x script.sh`, and then execute with `./script.sh`. The script will print the certificate(s) that have the specified issuer string.

Please note that this script assumes a format where each certificate starts with "-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----" and ends with "-----END CERTIFICATE-----", and has an "Issuer:" line to match the issuer. If your bundle follows a different format, you might need to modify the script.

Sure. The user interface of the new phone application is simple and easy to use. The main screen consists of a list of all the user's contacts. When a contact is selected, the application displays a list of all the recent messages that have been exchanged with that contact. The application also allows users to send new messages, make calls, and send photos and videos.

The application uses JSON data to store and retrieve user information. JSON is a lightweight data-interchange format that is easy to read and write. The application's JSON data is stored in a file on the user's device. The application uses a JSON parser to read and write the JSON data.

The following is a sample Java code implementation of one of the application's key features:

```java
public class ContactManager {

    private static final String CONTACTS_FILE = "contacts.json";

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // Load the contacts from the JSON file.
            List<Contact> contacts = loadContacts();

            // Display the contacts.
            displayContacts(contacts);

        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private static List<Contact> loadContacts() throws Exception {
        // Open the JSON file.
        File file = new File(CONTACTS_FILE);

        // Read the JSON data from the file.
        String jsonData = readFile(file);

        // Parse the JSON data into a List of Contact objects.
        return parseContacts(jsonData);
    }

    private static List<Contact> parseContacts(String jsonData) {
        // Create a JSON parser.
        JSONObject jsonObject = new JSONObject(jsonData);

        // Get the "contacts" array from the JSON object.
        List<Contact> contacts = jsonObject.getJSONArray("contacts").toList();

        // Return the list of Contact objects.
        return contacts;
    }

    private static void displayContacts(List<Contact> contacts) {
        // For each contact in the list, display the contact's name and phone number.
        for (Contact contact : contacts) {
            System.out.println(contact.getName() + " - " + contact.getPhoneNumber());
        }
    }

    private static String readFile(File file) throws Exception {
        // Open the file for reading.
        FileInputStream fileInputStream = new FileInputStream(file);

        // Create a byte array to store the contents of the file.
        byte[] bytes = new byte[(int) file.length()];

        // Read the contents of the file into the byte array.
        int bytesRead = fileInputStream.read(bytes);

        // Close the file input stream.
        fileInputStream.close();

        // Return the contents of the file as a string.
        return new String(bytes, 0, bytesRead);
    }
}
```

Here's a sample code in C++ for Raspberry Pi 4B to control GPIO pin 12 with hardware PWM using pigpio library.

```c++
#include <bcm2835.h>
#include <pigpio/pigpio.h>
#include <pwm.h>

int gpio_pin[] = {12}; // pin to control

void setup() {
  bcm2835_gpio_fsel(gpio_pin, BCM2835_GPIO_FSEL_OUTP);
}

void loop() {
  float freq = 500.0;
  pwmSetMode(0, GPIO_TIMER_MODE_PWM); //use timer 0
  pwmSetRange(0, 50.0, 1000.0); //max dutycycle 50%
  pwmSetClock(0, freq); //set to 500 Hz
  pwmSetRange(0, 0, freq); //zero offset
  pwmSetClock(0, 0);
  pwmSetRange(0, 100.0, freq);
  pwmStop(0);
  pwmSetRange(0, freq, freq); //pwm value 100 is required for hardware PWM
  pwmStop(0);
  pwmClearRange(0, 7); //add edge detect signal on rising edge detection of pin

  while(freq > 0.0) {
    int duty_milli = pwmSetDutyCycle(0, 1000.0 * freq / (1<<12));
    pwmStop(0);
  }
}

int main() {
  setup();
  rp4_setup(); //Set up Raspberry Pi4 io pins
  digitalWrite(12, 0); //Initialise GPIO pin mode (Pin 12 is now an output)
  delay(3000);
  loop();
  digitalWrite(12, 1); //Turn off Pin 12
  return 0;
}
```

