To achieve this, you can use the Google Places API in your Google Apps Script project to search for the place and obtain its latitude and longitude. Then, store these values in the respective columns in your Google Sheet. Here's a step-by-step guide to achieve this:

1. Enable and obtain an API key for Google Places API:

- Go to Google Cloud Console (https://console.cloud.google.com/).
- Create a new project or select an existing project.
- Search for "Places API" in the search bar and enable the API.
- Navigate to "Credentials" in the left sidebar, click "Create credentials", and select "API key". Save the generated API key.

2. Create an Apps Script project and paste the following code:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  const sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();
  const placeColumn = 1; // Column index where place names are stored (1 = column A)
  const latColumn = 2; // Column index where latitude should be stored (2 = column B)
  const lngColumn = 3; // Column index where longitude should be stored (3 = column C)

  // Replace YOUR_API_KEY with your actual Google Places API key.
  const apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY';

  const numRows = sheet.getLastRow();

  for (let i = 2; i <= numRows; i++) {
    const placeName = sheet.getRange(i, placeColumn).getValue();
    const placeDetails = getPlaceDetails(placeName, apiKey);
    if (placeDetails) {
      sheet.getRange(i, latColumn).setValue(placeDetails.latitude);
      sheet.getRange(i, lngColumn).setValue(placeDetails.longitude);
    }
  }
}

function getPlaceDetails(placeName, apiKey) {
  const apiUrl = 'https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/place/findplacefromtext/json';
  const queryParams = {
    input: encodeURIComponent(placeName),
    inputtype: 'textquery',
    fields: 'geometry/location',
    key: apiKey
  };
  const queryString = Object.entries(queryParams).map(([key, value]) => key + '=' + value).join('&');
  const url = apiUrl + '?' + queryString;

  const response = UrlFetchApp.fetch(url);
  const jsonResponse = JSON.parse(response.getContentText());

  if (jsonResponse.status === 'OK' && jsonResponse.candidates.length > 0) {
    const location = jsonResponse.candidates[0].geometry.location;
    return { latitude: location.lat, longitude: location.lng };
  } else {
    console.warn(`Could not find a place with name: ${placeName}`);
    return null;
  }
}
```

3. Replace `'YOUR_API_KEY'` in the code with your Google Places API key.

4. In your Google Sheet, fill the place names in the first column (or any other column, but make sure you update the `placeColumn` variable in the script).

5. Run the `searchPlace()` function from the script editor or set a trigger to run it.

This script assumes that the place names to be searched are in column A, latitude in column B, and longitude in column C. If you want to use different columns, adjust the `placeColumn`, `latColumn`, and `lngColumn` variable values in the script accordingly.

