Sure! Here's a well-commented Perl script that takes a user-defined array of numbers as input and calculates the sum of all the numbers in the array.

Please keep in mind that this script has been specifically designed according to your request, and it follows best practices for Perl coding style. Also, error checking is included to ensure the input only contains valid numeric values.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl

# Import strict and warnings modules for better coding practices
use strict;
use warnings;

# Prompt the user to enter numbers for the array (space-separated)
print "Please enter a space-separated list of numbers: ";
my $input = <STDIN>; # Read the input from the user
chomp $input; # Remove any trailing newline

# Split the input string into an array using whitespace as the delimiter
my @numbers = split(/\s+/, $input);

# Initialize the sum variable to store the sum of the array elements
my $sum = 0;

# Loop through the elements in the array
foreach my $num (@numbers) {
    # Check if the current element is a valid number
    if ($num =~ /^-?\d+(\.\d+)?$/) {
        # Add the numeric value to the sum
        $sum += $num;
    } else {
        # Display an error message for invalid input and exit with an error status
        print "Error: The input contains an invalid value: $num\n";
        exit 1;
    }
}

# Print the result
print "The sum of the numbers is: $sum\n";
```

This script should be executed in a terminal/command prompt, where the user will be prompted to enter a space-separated list of numbers. The script will then calculate the sum of all the numbers in the input array and print the result. If an invalid value is encountered, the script will display an error message and exit with an error status.

