Avalonia is a UI framework similar to WPF for creating cross-platform desktop applications using XAML and C#. To ensure that only one instance of an Avalonia application is running, you can use a mutex in your `Main` method. A named mutex creates a system-wide synchronization mechanism with a unique name that can be locked and unlocked across multiple processes.

Here's a simple example of how to use a named mutex to ensure only one running instance of an Avalonia application:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;
using Avalonia;
using Avalonia.Controls.ApplicationLifetimes;
using Avalonia.Markup.Xaml;

public class App : Application
{
    public override void Initialize() => AvaloniaXamlLoader.Load(this);

    [STAThread]
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        const string mutexName = "MyUniqueAvaloniaAppInstance";
        using (var mutex = new Mutex(true, mutexName, out bool createdNew))
        {
            if (createdNew)
            {
                var app = new App();
                var lifetime = new ClassicDesktopStyleApplicationLifetime
                {
                    Args = args
                };
                app.Attach(lifetime);
                lifetime.Run();
            }
            else
            {
                Console.WriteLine("An instance of this application is already running!");
                // Show a message or bring the existing instance to the foreground, if desired.
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Replace the `MyUniqueAvaloniaAppInstance` string with a unique identifier for your application. Make sure to use a guid or other unique value to prevent collisions with other applications.

After replacing the `APP.xaml.cs` file, your Avalonia project will have a unique instance of the application running at a time.

The @EnableAspectJAutoProxy annotation is used to enable AspectJ autoproxying in a Java Spring container configuration class. This annotation tells Spring to create proxy objects for all of the beans in the application that are annotated with @AspectJ. This can be useful for applying aspects to code that is not Spring-managed.

To use the @EnableAspectJAutoProxy annotation, you must first add the following dependency to your pom.xml file:

```
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-aspects</artifactId>
  <version>${spring.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

Then, you need to annotate your configuration class with @EnableAspectJAutoProxy:

```
@Configuration
@EnableAspectJAutoProxy
public class AppConfig {

}
```

Finally, you need to add the @AspectJ annotation to the classes that you want to apply aspects to. For example:

```
@AspectJ
public class MyAspect {

  @Pointcut("execution(* com.example.*.*(..))")
  public void pointcut() {}

  @Around("pointcut()")
  public Object around(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {
    System.out.println("Executing aspect");
    return pjp.proceed();
  }

}
```

When you run your application, Spring will create proxy objects for all of the beans in the application that are annotated with @AspectJ. These proxy objects will intercept all of the methods that are annotated with @Pointcut, and will apply the aspects that are defined in those methods.

