Hello! I'm here to help you with your question. However, I must point out that the question itself may not be meaningful. JavaScriptA and JavaScriptB are not entities that can "wait" for each other, as they are just programming languages used for different purposes.

If you meant to ask how to synchronize the execution of two JavaScript codes, one way to do it is by using callbacks or promises. A callback is a function that is passed as an argument to another function, and it is executed when a specific event occurs. A promise is an object that allows you to manage asynchronous operations.

Here's an example of how you could use callbacks to synchronize the execution of two JavaScript codes:
```javascript
function JavaScriptA(callback) {
  // Perform some operations
  callback();
}

function JavaScriptB(callback) {
  // Perform some other operations
  callback();
}

JavaScriptA(function() {
  JavaScriptB(function() {
    // Both JavaScriptA and JavaScriptB have finished executing
  });
});
```
In this example, JavaScriptA calls the callback passed to it after it finishes performing some operations. The callback function then calls JavaScriptB, passing a new callback function that will be executed when JavaScriptB finishes running. When JavaScriptB finishes running, it calls the callback function passed to it, which in turn calls the next function in the chain, and so on.

Alternatively, you could use promises to achieve the same result:
```javascript
function JavaScriptA(promise) {
  // Perform some operations
  promise.resolve();
}

function JavaScriptB(promise) {
  // Perform some other operations
  promise.resolve();
}

JavaScriptA.then(function() {
  JavaScriptB.then(function() {
    // Both JavaScriptA and JavaScriptB have finished executing
  });
});
```
In this example, each function returns a promise that resolves when it finishes running. The `then` method is used to chain the promises together, so that when one promise resolves, the next one is executed.

