In JavaScript, you can make an HTTP request using the `XMLHttpRequest` object or the more modern `Fetch` API. I'll show you examples of both.

**1. Using `XMLHttpRequest`:**

```javascript
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.onreadystatechange = function() {
    if (xhr.readyState === 4 && xhr.status === 200) {
        console.log(xhr.responseText);
    }
};
xhr.open("GET", "https://api.example.com/data", true);
xhr.send();
```

**2. Using `Fetch` API:**

```javascript
fetch("https://api.example.com/data")
  .then(response => {
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error("There was an error with the request");
    }
    return response.json(); // or response.text() for plain text
  })
  .then(data => {
    console.log(data);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error("Error:", error);
  });
```

The `Fetch` API returns a Promise that resolves to the response of the request. You can then process the response using `.json()` for JSON data, `.text()` for plain text, or other methods for different formats. Remember that `Fetch` is a modern API and might not work on older browsers, so you might need a polyfill or use `XMLHttpRequest` on older browsers to ensure compatibility.

