I understand that you want to detect when a user opens a window containing a video that is playing on your Samsung MDN on an Android phone. However, I must point out that using accessibility features to monitor user behavior without their consent may raise privacy concerns.

Instead, I suggest you explore alternative solutions that prioritize user privacy and consent. For instance, you could consider implementing a feature that allows users to opt-in to video detection, or you could use machine learning algorithms to detect video playback based on user behavior, while still respecting their privacy.

If you're still interested in using accessibility features, you can use the Android accessibility API to detect window changes and determine if a video is playing. You can do this by implementing a broadcast receiver that listens for window change events and uses the accessibility API to check if a video is playing in the newly focused window.

Here's a high-level overview of how you can implement this:

1. Create a broadcast receiver that listens for window change events:
```java
// In your AndroidManifest.xml file
<receiver android:name=".WindowChangeReceiver">
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.WINDOW_CHANGE" />
    </intent-filter>
</receiver>
```
1. In the broadcast receiver, use the accessibility API to check if a video is playing in the newly focused window:
```java
// In your WindowChangeReceiver class
public class WindowChangeReceiver extends BroadcastReceiver {
    @Override
    public void onReceive(Context context, Intent intent) {
        String packageName = context.getPackageName();
        String className = context.getClass().getSimpleName();

        // Check if the window change event is for a video playback app
        if (packageName.equals("com.example.videoplayer") && className.equals("VideoPlayerActivity")) {
            // Use the accessibility API to check if a video is playing
            AccessibilityManager accessibilityManager = (AccessibilityManager) context.getSystemService(Context.ACCESSIBILITY_SERVICE);
            AccessibilityEvent event = accessibilityManager.getLastEvent();

            if (event != null && event.getEventType() == AccessibilityEvent.TYPE_WINDOW_STATE_CHANGED) {
                // Extract the window ID from the event
                int windowId = event.getWindowId();

                // Get the window from the accessibility service
                AccessibilityWindowInfo windowInfo = accessibilityManager.getWindow(windowId);

                // Check if the window is a video playback window
                if (windowInfo.getClassName().equals("VideoPlayerActivity")) {
                    // Notify the user that a video is playing
                    // ...
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Please keep in mind that this code is just a starting point and may require additional modifications to work correctly in your specific use case.

Remember to always use the accessibility features ethically and with the user's consent. It's essential to prioritize user privacy and security when developing applications that use accessibility features.

Sure, I'd be happy to help you with that! Here's a basic outline of how you could create an interactive map using the Google Maps API and JavaScript:

1. First, you'll need to create a Google Maps API key. You can do this by going to the Google Cloud Console and signing up for a free account. Once you have an account, you can create a new project and enable the Google Maps JavaScript API. This will give you an API key that you'll need to include in your code.
2. Next, you'll need to include the Google Maps JavaScript API library in your HTML file. You can do this by adding the following script tag to the head of your HTML document:
```
<script src="https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/js?key=YOUR_API_KEY"></script>
```
3. Now, you'll need to create a div element in your HTML file where the map will be displayed. You can do this by adding the following code to the body of your HTML document:
```
<div id="map"></div>
```
4. In your JavaScript file, you'll need to create a new Google Map object and pass it the div element where the map will be displayed. You can do this by adding the following code:
```
const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById('map'), {
  center: {lat: 37.7749, lng: -122.4194},
  zoom: 13
});
```
This will create a new map object and center it on San Francisco, California.

5. Next, you'll need to add markers to the map. You can do this by creating a new array of marker objects and adding them to the map using the `map.add()` method. Here's an example of how you could add a marker for the Golden Gate Bridge:
```
const marker = new google.maps.Marker({
  position: {lat: 37.7749, lng: -122.4194},
  map: map,
  title: 'Golden Gate Bridge'
});
```
6. Finally, you'll need to add an event listener to the marker so that it displays additional information when the user clicks on it. You can do this by adding the following code:
```
google.maps.event.addListener(marker, 'click', function() {
  // Display additional information about the Golden Gate Bridge
  console.log('Golden Gate Bridge clicked!');
});
```
7. To allow users to zoom in and out and scroll to explore different areas of the map, you can add the following code:
```
map. zoomin();
map.zoomout();
map.pan();
```
8. To customize the style of the map to fit the overall design aesthetic of the web page, you can use the Google Maps Styled Maps feature. This allows you to change the colors, fonts, and other styling elements of the map. You can do this by creating a new styles object and passing it to the `map.setOptions()` method. Here's an example of how you could change the color of the map's roads and buildings:
```
const styles = [
  {
    featureType: 'road',
    elementType: 'geometry',
    stylers: [
      {color: '#f0c999'}
    ]
  },
  {
    featureType: 'building',
    elementType: 'geometry',
    stylers: [
      {color: '#ff9933'}
    ]
  }
];
map.setOptions({styles: styles});
```
