Yes, you can obtain a token from the callback and switch to the current object thread. In most programming languages and libraries, callbacks are used to handle asynchronous functions. When a reply is received, the callback is invoked, and the token or data can be extracted to work with the current object thread.

For example, in JavaScript, using the Fetch API, a callback can be used to obtain a token. Assume you have a function called `getToken` that fetches the token from an API:

```javascript
function getToken(callback) {
  fetch('https://api.example.com/token')
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => callback(data.token));
}

// To use the token
