To use Terratest for running Terraform code from a directory and then validate whether there is a running container in an Azure Web App, you can follow these steps. First, create a Go program for the test with the correct imports, initial configurations, and the `TestTerraformAzureContainerWebApp` function:

```go
package test

import (
    "fmt"
    "testing"

    "github.com/gruntwork-io/terratest/modules/azure"
    "github.com/gruntwork-io/terratest/modules/random"
    "github.com/gruntwork-io/terratest/modules/terraform"
    "github.com/stretchr/testify/assert"
    "github.com/stretchr/testify/require"
)

func TestTerraformAzureContainerWebApp(t *testing.T) {
    t.Parallel()

    // Load the target directory for the Terraform code
    terraformDirectory := "../my_terraform_code"

    // Randomly generate a unique identifier for this test
    uniqueID := random.UniqueId()

    // Configure the Terraform options with the terraformDirectory, uniqueID, and the backend configuration
    terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
        TerraformDir: terraformDirectory,
        Vars: map[string]interface{}{
            "unique_id": uniqueID,
        },
    }

    // Defer terraform destroy at the end of the test to ensure resources are destroyed
    defer terraform.Destroy(t, terraformOptions)

    // Run terraform init and apply to create the resources
    terraform.InitAndApply(t, terraformOptions)

    // Extract output variables from the Terraform state
    resourceGroupName := terraform.Output(t, terraformOptions, "resource_group_name")
    webAppName := terraform.Output(t, terraformOptions, "web_app_name")

    // Verify whether a container with the correct settings is running in the Azure Web App
    verifyRunningContainerInAzureWebApp(t, resourceGroupName, webAppName)
}

func verifyRunningContainerInAzureWebApp(t *testing.T, resourceGroupName string, webAppName string) {
    // Provide your subscription ID, client ID, client secret, and tenant ID environment variables
    subscriptionID := "" // Your subscription ID

    // Get the details of the Azure Web App
    webApp := azure.GetAppService(t, subscriptionID, resourceGroupName, webAppName)

    // Check if there is a container running in the Web App
    assert.True(t, webApp.SiteConfig.LinuxFxVersion != nil, "The Azure Web App should have a container running.")
}
```

This simple `TestTerraformAzureContainerWebApp()` function will load Terraform configurations from a directory, apply them to deploy an Azure Web App with container support, and verify if there is a running container in the Web App.

To run the test, set the appropriate environment variables in your machine or CI/CD tool to provide your Azure subscription, client ID, client secret, and tenant ID:

```
$ export ARM_CLIENT_ID="YOUR_AZURE_CLIENT_ID"
$ export ARM_CLIENT_SECRET="YOUR_AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET"
$ export ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID="YOUR_AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID"
$ export ARM_TENANT_ID="YOUR_AZURE_TENANT_ID"
```

Finally, run the test using the Go testing tool:

```
$ go test -v ./test
```

This will run the Terratest code, apply the Terraform configurations, and validate if there is a running container in the Azure Web App.

