To apply inheritance in C# with dynamic type, you can use the `dynamic` keyword. The `dynamic` keyword allows you to use a variable without specifying its type. This can be useful when you are working with a set of possible types, such as classes or interfaces.

For example, you could create a class called `Animal` and an interface called `IAnimal`. The `Animal` class would have a method called `Speak()`, and the `IAnimal` interface would have a method called `Eat()`.

You could then create a variable called `animal` and assign it a value of type `dynamic`. You could then call the `Speak()` method on the `animal` variable, even though you don't know what type the `animal` variable is.


```c#
public class Animal
{
    public void Speak()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("I am an animal!");
    }
}

public interface IAnimal
{
    void Eat();
}

dynamic animal = new Animal();
animal.Speak();
```

In this example, the `Speak()` method will be called on the `animal` variable, even though the `animal` variable is of type `dynamic`. This is because the `Animal` class implements the `IAnimal` interface, and the `IAnimal` interface has a method called `Eat()`.

You can also use the `dynamic` keyword to create generic types. For example, you could create a generic type called `List` that can hold any type of object.


```c#
public class List<T> where T : class
{
    private List<T> list;

    public void Add(T item)
    {
        list.Add(item);
    }

    public T Get(int index)
    {
        return list[index];
    }
}
```

In this example, the `List` generic type can hold any type of object. This is because the `List` generic type uses the `dynamic` keyword.

The `dynamic` keyword can be a powerful tool, but it is important to use it carefully. If you use the `dynamic` keyword incorrectly, it can make your code difficult to maintain and debug.

In React Native, you can use the Flatpickr component to create a date picker. To do this, you need to import the Flatpickr component and use it in your component. You also need to provide some props to the Flatpickr component, such as the initial date and the date format.

Here is an example of how to use Flatpickr to create a date picker in React Native:
```
import React, { Component } from 'react';
import Flatpickr from 'flatpickr';

class App extends Component {
state = {
  date: new Date()
};

render() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Flatpickr
        onChange={(date) => this.setState({ date })}
        value={this.state.date}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
}

export default App;
```
The Flatpickr component will render a date picker inside the component. The date picker will have the initial date set to the current date and will be updated when the user changes the date.

You can also customize the look and feel of the date picker by providing different props to the Flatpickr component. For example, you can change the theme of the date picker by providing the theme prop.

Here is an example of how to change the theme of the date picker:
```
import React, { Component } from 'react';
import Flatpickr from 'flatpickr';

class App extends Component {
state = {
  date: new Date()
};

render() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Flatpickr
        onChange={(date) => this.setState({ date })}
        value={this.state.date}
        theme='dark'
      />
    </div>
  );
}
}

export default App;
```
This will change the theme of the date picker to dark mode.

