Here is a possible solution to find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array with a time complexity of O(n):
```
function findSecondLargest( arr[] ): int {
  // Special case for empty array
  if (arr.length == 0) {
    return -1;
  }

  // Sort the array in ascending order
  arr.sort(function(a, b){return b - a});

  // Find the pivot element in the sorted array
  var i = 0;
  var maxSize = 2;
  for (var j = 0; j < arr.length; j++) {
      if (arr[j] != arr[i] && arr[j] - arr[i] > maxSize) {
          maxSize = arr[j] - arr[i];
          i = j;
      }
  }

  // Find the second largest integer element
  var secondIndex = i-1;
  for (var k = i-2; k >= 0; k--) {
      if (arr[k]+1 == arr[i] && arr[k]+1 != maxSize) {
          secondIndex = k;
          break;
      }
  }
  return arr[secondIndex];
}
```
Explanation:
- First, we sort the array in ascending order. Then, we find the pivot element by comparing the values on either side of it. If the difference is greater than the current maximum size, we update the maximum size and the index of the element.
- After this, we can find the second largest integer element by checking whether any adjacent elements in the unsorted array have a difference of one. If this is the case, we update the secondIndex variable to the previous element in the array.
- If there is no adjacent element with a difference of one, we are done finding the second largest size.
- The function ends by returning the second largest integer element found in the array.

Yes, Spring maps key values for requestbody object. When a request is received by Spring, the request body is converted into a Java object using the @RequestBody annotation. The key values of the request body are mapped to the properties of the Java object. This allows you to access the data from the request body easily and efficiently.

For example, if you have a request body with the following JSON payload:

```
{
  "name": "John Doe",
  "age": 30,
  "address": "123 Main Street, Anytown, CA 91234"
}
```

You can map the key values of the request body to the following Java object:

```
public class User {

  private String name;
  private int age;
  private String address;

  public String getName() {
    return name;
  }

  public void setName(String name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  public int getAge() {
    return age;
  }

  public void setAge(int age) {
    this.age = age;
  }

  public String getAddress() {
    return address;
  }

  public void setAddress(String address) {
    this.address = address;
  }
}
```

You can then access the data from the request body using the getter and setter methods of the User object. For example, to get the name of the user, you would use the following code:

```
String name = user.getName();
```

To set the name of the user, you would use the following code:

```
user.setName("Jane Doe");
```

The @RequestBody annotation is a powerful tool that allows you to easily access the data from the request body. It is often used in conjunction with Spring MVC controllers to map the request body to Java objects.

Linear search and binary search are both search algorithms that can be used to find a value in a list. Linear search is a simpler algorithm that scans the list from beginning to end until the desired value is found. Binary search is a more efficient algorithm that divides the list in half at each step and only needs to compare the desired value to the middle element of the list.

In Go code, linear search can be implemented as follows:

```func linearSearch(list []int, value int) int {
    for i := 0; i < len(list); i++ {
        if list[i] == value {
            return i
        }
    }
    return -1
}
```

Binary search can be implemented as follows:

```func binarySearch(list []int, value int) int {
    low := 0
    high := len(list) - 1
    for low <= high {
        mid := (low + high) / 2
        if list[mid] == value {
            return mid
        } else if list[mid] < value {
            low = mid + 1
        } else {
            high = mid - 1
        }
    }
    return -1
}
```

An example of a problem that can be solved using both linear search and binary search is finding a value in a sorted list. To find the value 5 in the list [1, 3, 5, 7, 9], linear search would need to compare 5 to each element of the list, one by one, until it found the match. This would take 5 comparisons. Binary search, on the other hand, would only need to compare 5 to the middle element of the list, then to the middle element of the remaining half of the list, and so on. This would take only 3 comparisons.

The runtime complexity of linear search is $O(n)$, where $n$ is the number of elements in the list. The runtime complexity of binary search is $O(\log n)$, where $n$ is the number of elements in the list. This means that binary search is more efficient than linear search for large lists.

In general, binary search is a more efficient algorithm than linear search for finding values in sorted lists. However, linear search is simpler to implement and can be faster for small lists.

