Creating a program that can have an alliterative conversation is an interesting idea! Here's a simple example using C++. This code snippet doesn't cover all possibilities but serves as a starting point that can be extended and improved. The sample code uses a predetermined set of alliterative words. You can expand the sets of words to cover more letters and create more meaningful conversations.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <ctime>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdlib>

std::string pick_random_word(const std::vector<std::string> &words) {
    int random_index = rand() % words.size();
    return words[random_index];
}

std::string generate_alliterative_sentence(const std::vector<std::string> &words) {
    return pick_random_word(words) + " " + pick_random_word(words) + " " + pick_random_word(words);
}

int main() {
    srand(time(0));

    const std::vector<std::vector<std::string>> alliterative_words_sets = {
        {"apple", "ant", "airplane"},
        {"bear", "bee", "banana"},
        {"cat", "cookie", "candy"},
        {"dog", "dolphin", "door"},
        {"elephant", "ear", "elbow"},
        {"frog", "flower", "fun"},
    };

    const int character_count = 2;
    const int conversation_length = 4;

    const std::string characters[character_count] = {"Alice:", "Bob:"};

    for (int round = 0; round < conversation_length; ++round) {
        int random_set_index = rand() % alliterative_words_sets.size();

        for (int i = 0; i < character_count; ++i) {
            std::cout << characters[i] << " ";
            std::cout << generate_alliterative_sentence(alliterative_words_sets[random_set_index]) << std::endl;
        }

        std::cout << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code first sets up the alliterative word sets, each part of the conversation, and the characters. It then generates a conversation by picking a random set of words, generating an alliterative sentence for each character, and printing the result. The conversation continues for as many rounds as specified by the `conversation_length` variable.

Keep in mind that generating semantically meaningful alliterative sentences requires more advanced natural language processing and might not be feasible using simplistic approaches.

