To identify emotions in text, you can use the `Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language.TextAnalytics` NuGet package. It provides sentiment analysis using Azure Text Analytics API, which can classify the emotions in your text.

First, you need to set up an Azure Text Analytics API resource. Follow the instructions given in this link to set it up: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cognitive-services/text-analytics/how-tos/text-analytics-how-to-call-api

Once you have set up the resource, here's a code snippet to help you analyze the emotions in your text:

1. Install the NuGet package: 
```
Install-Package Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language.TextAnalytics -Version 5.1.0
```

2. Add the following namespaces:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language.TextAnalytics;
using Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language.TextAnalytics.Models;
```

3. Here's the code to analyze the emotion:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language.TextAnalytics;
using Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language.TextAnalytics.Models;

public class EmotionAnalyzer
{
    private static readonly string SubscriptionKey = "YOUR-API-KEY";
    private static readonly string Endpoint = "https://<RESOURCE-NAME>.cognitiveservices.azure.com/";

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
-        string text = "Despite their hard work and dedication, their colleague was promoted over them once again.";
        string emotion = AnalyzeEmotion(text);
        Console.WriteLine($"Detected emotion: {emotion}");
    }

    public static string AnalyzeEmotion(string text)
    {
        var client = new TextAnalyticsClient(new ApiKeyServiceClientCredentials(SubscriptionKey))
        {
            Endpoint = Endpoint
        };

        SentimentResult sentimentResult = client.Sentiment(text, "en");
        double positiveScore = sentimentResult.DocumentSentiment.Positive;
        double negativeScore = sentimentResult.DocumentSentiment.Negative;

        return (negativeScore > positiveScore) ? "Resentment" : "Envy";
    }

    private class ApiKeyServiceClientCredentials : IServiceClientCredentials
    {
        private readonly string subscriptionKey;

        public ApiKeyServiceClientCredentials(string subscriptionKey)
        {
            this.subscriptionKey = subscriptionKey ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(subscriptionKey));
        }

        public Task ProcessHttpRequestAsync(HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
        {
            if (request == null)
            {
                throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(request));
            }
            request.Headers.Add("Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key", subscriptionKey);
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }
    }
}
```

This example takes a given text and uses the Text Analytics API to analyze the sentiment. It then outputs the emotion as either "Envy" or "Resentment". Please note that the sample code do not classify emotions specifically as 'Envy', 'Jealousy', or 'Resentment', you can modify the emotion classification method or train a more sophisticated machine learning model to better suit your needs.

Make sure to replace "YOUR-API-KEY" with your actual API key and "<RESOURCE-NAME>" with the name of the resource you created on Azure.

