public class ThirtyDaySalesAverage : IAverageDailySales
{
 private readonly ISalesHistory \_salesHistory;

 public ThirtyDaySalesAverage(ISalesHistory salesHistory)
 {
 \_salesHistory = salesHistory;
 }

 public double ForProduct(Product product)
 {
 DateTime endDate = DateTime.Now;
 DateTime startDate = endDate.AddDays(-30);
 return \_salesHistory.WithinDateRange(product.Id, startDate, endDate)/ 30.0;
 }
}

The provided MATLAB script (diceSimulation.m) runs a simulation of rolling six, 6-sided dice and calculating the sum. The simulation is repeated 1,000,000 times to create a histogram of the probability distribution as shown below.

The code produces the correct result, but can be improved to run faster. Rewrite the script such that the simulation produces the same results with 1,000,000 trials, a histogram of the results is created, and a faster execution time.

Here is the script "diceSimulation.m":

%% Simulate the sum of rolling 6 dice 1e6 times.

numSims = 1e6;
for i = 1:numSims

 % Create 6 random integers in the range [1, 6]
 dice = randi([1, 6], 1, 6);

 % Sum the six integers and store the result
 diceSum(i) = sum(dice);
end

histogram(diceSum);

output:
You can improve the script's efficiency by using vectorized operations on matrices to generate all dice rolls at once and sum them along one dimension. Here's an optimized version of the script:
```matlab
%% Simulate the sum of rolling 6 dice 1e6 times, optimized.

numSims = 1e6;

% Create a matrix with 1e6 rows and 6 columns of random integers in the range [1, 6]
dice = randi([1, 6], numSims, 6);

% Sum the six integers along each row and store the results
diceSum = sum(dice, 2);

histogram(diceSum);
```
This version of the script generates all the dice rolls at once and then applies a vectorized sum operation along the rows, which should lead to faster execution times compared to the original code.

