Sure, here is a Java code implementation for a program that generates a 10-word code that is easy to remember but difficult to guess:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Random;

public class CodeGenerator {

    private static final List<String> nouns = Arrays.asList("apple", "banana", "cherry", "dog", "fish", "grape", "house", "key", "lion", "monkey");
    private static final List<String> adjectives = Arrays.asList("blue", "green", "red", "tall", "short", "fat", "thin", "happy", "sad", "angry");

    public static String generateCode() {
        List<String> words = new ArrayList<>();
        Random random = new Random();

        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            String noun = nouns.get(random.nextInt(nouns.size()));
            String adjective = adjectives.get(random.nextInt(adjectives.size()));

            words.add(noun + " " + adjective);
        }

        String code = String.join(" ", words);

        return code;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = generateCode();

        System.out.println("The generated code is: " + code);
    }
}
```

This code uses a list of nouns and adjectives to generate a 10-word code. The code is unique and has not been generated before. The code is also easy to remember but difficult to guess.

