Regarding your first question, finding the longest continuous increasing subarray in a given array is a common task, and there are several algorithms that can be used to achieve this. One optimized approach is to use a combination of two pointers, one pointing to the starting index of the subarray and the other pointing to the ending index.

Here's a step-by-step approach to find the longest continuous increasing subarray in an array:

1. Initialize two pointers, `start` and `end`, both pointing to the first element of the array.
2. Compare the current element with the previous one. If the current element is greater than the previous one, increase `end` to point to the current element.
3. If the current element is less than or equal to the previous one, move `start` to the current element and set `end` to the previous element.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until the end of the array is reached.
5. The difference between `start` and `end` gives the length of the longest continuous increasing subarray.

To optimize this algorithm, you can use techniques such as using a single pointer and keeping track of the minimum element in the subarray, or using a sliding window approach.

As for your second question, to find not only the longest increasing subarray but also the second longest one, you can simply use two pointers as before, but this time keep track of two separate lengths for the longest and second-longest subarrays. You can keep track of the minimum element in the subarray and use a sliding window approach to find the second-longest subarray.

Here's an example C# implementation that can handle both situations:
```csharp
public static int[] FindLongestAndSecondLongestIncreasingSubarrays(int[] myArray)
{
    int[] longest = new int[1];
    int[] secondLongest = new int[1];
    int start = 0;
    int end = 0;
    int minElement = int.MaxValue;

    for (int i = 0; i < myArray.Length; i++)
    {
        if (myArray[i] > minElement)
        {
            minElement = myArray[i];
            start = i;
        }
        else if (myArray[i] == minElement)
        {
            end = i;
            if (end - start + 1 > longest[0])
            {
                longest[0] = end - start + 1;
                longest[1] = minElement;
            }
            else if (end - start + 1 == longest[0])
            {
                secondLongest[0] = end - start + 1;
                secondLongest[1] = minElement;
            }
            start = i + 1;
        }
        else
        {
            end = i + 1;
        }
    }

    return new int[] { longest[0], secondLongest[0] };
}
```
This implementation uses two pointers, `start` and `end`, to keep track of the longest and second-longest subarrays, respectively. It also keeps track of the minimum element in the subarray and uses a sliding window approach to find the second-longest subarray. The `FindLongestAndSecondLongestIncreasingSubarrays` method returns an array with two elements, representing the lengths of the longest and second-longest increasing subarrays, respectively.

Please note that this implementation has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(1), where n is the length of the input array.

