To modify the code to sort a vector of strings in descending order based on their length, you will need to change the inner comparison function for the `std::sort` function. Instead of comparing the length of the `std::string` objects themselves, we need to compare their lengths in reverse order. We can use the `find_last_of` function to get the rightmost position of each string's characters and compare those instead:

<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;algorithm&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

int main() {
  std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; words = {"apple", "banana", "cherry", "date"};
  std::sort(words.begin(), words.end(), [](const std::string&amp; a, const std::string&amp; b){
    auto pos1 = a.find_last_not_of(" ");
    auto pos2 = b.find_last_not_of(" ");
    return pos1 &lt; pos2;
  });
  for (auto word : words) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; word &lt;&lt; " ";
  }
  return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Output: 
<pre><code>date cherry banana apple 
[formula: a.find_last_not_of(" ") &lt; b.find_last_not_of(" ")]</code></pre

