Hello! As a top-tier AI assistant, I'm happy to help you with your question. To create the plot of the function y = x^2 - 2x + 1 for values of x from -5 to 5, you can use the following Matlab code:
```scss
% Define the function
f = @(x) x.^2 - 2*x + 1;

% Define the limits of integration
xmin = -5;
xmax = 5;

% Define the grid of points
N = 100;
dx = (xmax - xmin) / (N - 1);
x = xmin:dx:xmax;

% Evaluate the function at each point in the grid
y = f(x);

% Plot the function
plot(x, y);

% Annotate the plot with the coordinates of the vertex and the x-intercepts
text(x == 0, y == -2, 'Vertex', 'Color','red');
text(x == -5, y == -7, 'X-intercept -5', 'Color', 'blue');
text(x == 5, y == 3, 'X-intercept 5', 'Color', 'blue');
```
In this code, we first define the function `f(x) = x^2 - 2x + 1` using the anonymous function syntax. We then define the limits of integration `xmin` and `xmax`, and create a grid of `N` equally spaced points in the interval `[xmin, xmax]`. We evaluate the function at each point in the grid, and store the results in the `y` array.

Next, we plot the function using the `plot()` function, and add annotations to the plot using the `text()` function. We label the vertex of the function (which has coordinates `(0, -2)`) and the x-intercepts (which have coordinates `(5, 3)` and `(5, -7)`).

