No, this statement is not correct. Let me clarify the usage of @RequestParam and @ResponseBody in Spring MVC.

Both GET and POST requests can use @RequestParam. It is used to bind request parameters to a method parameter in your controller. You can use it with any HTTP request method, such as GET, POST, PUT, or DELETE, as long as the parameter is sent in the request.

Example usage in a controller method:

```java
@RequestMapping(value="/example", method=RequestMethod.GET)
public String example(@RequestParam("name") String name) {
    // process the 'name' parameter
}
```

Regarding @ResponseBody, its purpose is entirely different. It is used to indicate that the return value of a controller method should be directly used as the response body and not be processed further to resolve a view. It is not limited to POST method but can be used with any type of request.

When using @ResponseBody, the response will be converted to the requested media type (e.g., JSON, XML) using an appropriate message converter configured in Spring.

Example usage:

```java
@RequestMapping(value="/example", method=RequestMethod.GET)
@ResponseBody
public CustomObject example() {
    CustomObject customObject = new CustomObject();
    // set attributes for 'customObject'
    return customObject; // This object will be converted to the requested media type (e.g., JSON) and sent as the response body.
}
```

In short, both @RequestParam and @ResponseBody can be used with various request methods, and their purpose is different, as explained above.

Puppeteer is a Node.js library that allows you to control headless Chrome or Chromium browsers. It can be used to automate tasks that you would normally do with a keyboard and mouse.

To make a web crawler on Node.js using Puppeteer, you will need to:

1. Install Puppeteer.
2. Create a new Node.js project.
3. Create a file called `index.js`.
4. Import the `puppeteer` library.
5. Create a function to scrape a website.
6. Call the scraping function.
7. Handle errors.

Here is an example of a web crawler that uses Puppeteer to scrape the website `https://www.google.com`:

```const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

async function scrape(url) {
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto(url);
  const title = await page.title();
  const links = await page.evaluate(() => document.querySelectorAll('a'));
  for (const link of links) {
    console.log(link.href);
  }
  await browser.close();
}

scrape('https://www.google.com');
```

This code will open the Google website in a headless Chrome browser and scrape the title of the page and all of the links. The results will be printed to the console.

You can use Puppeteer to scrape any website that you can open in a browser. To do this, you will need to replace the `url` parameter in the `scrape` function with the URL of the website that you want to scrape.

Puppeteer is a powerful tool that can be used to automate a variety of tasks. If you need to scrape a website, Puppeteer is a good choice.

