To achieve this, you can create a custom script that uses both Bootstrap 5's progress bar component and JavaScript's `setTimeout` function to update the progress bar and display the corresponding messages at appropriate times. Here's a general outline of the implementation:

1. Create a container for the progress bar and message text in your HTML file:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <!-- Add Bootstrap CSS link here -->
</head>
<body>
  <div class="progress" style="height: 30px;">
    <div id="progressBar" class="progress-bar" style="width: 0;"></div>
  </div>
  <div id="progressMessage" class="mt-3"></div>

  <!-- Add jQuery and Bootstrap JS links here -->
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

2. In your `script.js` file, create a function to update the progress bar and display the corresponding message.

```javascript
const progressBar = document.getElementById('progressBar');
const progressMessage = document.getElementById('progressMessage');

const steps = [
  {duration: 10000, message: 'Warming up servers'},
  {duration: 15000, message: 'Establishing secure connection'},
  {duration: 20000, message: 'Preparing the input'},
  {duration: 165000, message: 'Running Prediction Model'},
  // Add more steps as needed
];

function updateProgress() {
  let totalTime = steps.reduce((acc, {duration}) => acc + duration, 0);
  if (totalTime < 180000) {
    console.warn("Total duration is less than 3 minutes. Adjust step durations to add up to 3 minutes.");
  }
  let accumulatedDuration = 0;

  for (const [index, {duration, message}] of steps.entries()) {
    setTimeout(() => {
      progressMessage.innerText = message;
      progressBar.style.transition = `width ${duration}ms linear`;
      accumulatedDuration += duration;
      progressBar.style.width = `${(accumulatedDuration / totalTime) * 100}%`;
      if (index === steps.length - 1) {
        setTimeout(() => {
          progressBar.style.width = '0%';
          progressMessage.innerText = '';
        }, duration);
      }
    }, accumulatedDuration);
  }
}

updateProgress();
```

This script initializes the progress bar, updates its width and message based on the duration provided in the `steps` array. Make sure the total duration adds up to 3 minutes (180000 milliseconds).

Remember to include the Bootstrap CSS, jQuery, and Bootstrap JS libraries in your HTML file. With this implementation, the progress bar should progress smoothly for 3 minutes and display each message according to the specified durations.

