    String key = "";
    if (flower.startsWith("Li")) {
        key = "Lilac";
    } else if (flower.startsWith("Da")) {
        key = "Daisy";
    } else if (flower.startsWith("Ma")) {
        key = "Marigold";
    } else if (flower.startsWith("Ch")) {
        key = "Chrysanthemum";
    }
    if (categories.containsKey(key)) {
        categories.get(key).add(flower);
    } else {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<String>();
        list.add(flower);
        categories.put(key, list);
    }
}
System.out.println(categories);
This code uses a HashMap to store the categories as keys and an ArrayList of flowers in each category as values. It also uses the startsWith method to check the first two letters of each flower's name.
How can I further optimize this code for performance and readability?

output:
To improve the performance and readability of the Java code, you can use a switch statement and Java 8 stream features. The optimized Java code is shown below:

```Java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class FlowerCategorizer {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] flowersArr = {"Lilac", "Daisy", "Marigold", "Chrysanthemum"};
        List<String> flowers = Arrays.asList(flowersArr);
        Map<String, List<String>> categories = new HashMap<>();

        flowers.forEach(flower -> {
            String key = getKey(flower);
            categories.putIfAbsent(key, new ArrayList<>());
            categories.get(key).add(flower);
        });

        System.out.println(categories);
    }

    private static String getKey(String flower) {
        String key;
        switch (flower.substring(0, 2)) {
            case "Li":
                key = "Lilac";
                break;
            case "Da":
                key = "Daisy";
                break;
            case "Ma":
                key = "Marigold";
                break;
            case "Ch":
                key = "Chrysanthemum";
                break;
            default:
                key = "Other";
                break;
        }
        return key;
    }
}
```

The optimized code incorporates the following improvements:

1. Use a Java List, `List<String>`, instead of a raw array `String[]`, which provides a more flexible interface for working with collections.
2. Replace the if-else statements with a switch statement to make it more readable and easier to maintain.
3. Create a separate method `getKey()` to get the category key from a flower's name, which increases the readability and modularity of the code.
4. Use Java 8's `forEach()` method to iterate over the flowers and populate the categories map.
5. Use the `putIfAbsent()` method to simplify the process of initializing and adding new categories to the map.

