To represent a differential equation in LaTeX, you can use the `display` environment and the `\diff` command from the `esdiff` package.

Here's an example of how to represent a simple differential equation:

\begin{code}
\begin{display math}
\diff{y}{x} = f(x,y)
\end{display math}
\end{code}

This will display the differential equation with proper formatting. You can also use the ` Physics` package which provides the `\dDiff` command to typeset differential equations.

\begin{code}
\begin{display math}
\dDiff{y}{x} = f(x,y)
\end{display math}
