Converting epoch time to EXIF DateTimeOriginal format in JavaScript can be achieved using the `Date` object. The EXIF DateTimeOriginal format is "YYYY:MM:DD HH:mm:ss".

Here's a function to convert epoch time to the desired format:

```javascript
function epochToExifDateTime(epoch) {
  const date = new Date(epoch * 1000); // Convert to milliseconds
  const year = date.getFullYear();
  const month = String(date.getMonth() + 1).padStart(2, '0');
  const day = String(date.getDate()).padStart(2, '0');
  const hours = String(date.getHours()).padStart(2, '0');
  const minutes = String(date.getMinutes()).padStart(2, '0');
  const seconds = String(date.getSeconds()).padStart(2, '0');

  return `${year}:${month}:${day} ${hours}:${minutes}:${seconds}`;
}

// Example usage:
const epochTime = 1633045867; // Example epoch time
console.log(epochToExifDateTime(epochTime));
// Output: "2021:10:01 03:24:27" (depending on your timezone)
```

This function first converts the epoch time to a `Date` object, then extracts the components (year, month, day, etc.), and formats them to yield the EXIF DateTimeOriginal format. Remember to multiply the epoch time by 1000, since JavaScript `Date` object works with milliseconds.

