To push the variable `mnpStatus` into the `registrations` state in React, you can use the `push()` method. Here's an example:
```javascript
checkMNPStatusAPI(phoneNo, index) {
console.log("mnpphoneno: " + mnpPhoneNo);
const headers = new Headers();
headers.append("Authorization", "Bearer " + user.getAccessToken());
fetch(api.checkPortInStatus(user.getProductCode(), mnpPhoneNo), {
headers: headers,
method: 'GET'
})
.then(r => r.json())
.then(rjson => {
this.state.registrations.push({...this.state.registrations[index], mnpStatus: rjson.status });
})
```
In the above code, `this.state.registrations` is an array of objects that represent the registrations. The `push()` method is used to add a new object to the end of the array, with the `mnpStatus` property set to the value returned from the API call.
You can also use `this.state.registrations.unshift()` method to push the new object to the beginning of the array.
You can also use `setState()` method to update the state, but it's important to be aware of the performance implications of doing so, as it can cause a re-render of the component.
It's also worth noting that in React, state is immutable, so you should not directly modify the state object. Instead, you should create a new object with the updated properties and use the `setState()` method to update the state.
