cd <project-name>
```

3. Inside the `components` directory, create a new file called `CityImage.vue`.

4. In `CityImage.vue`, add the following code:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <img v-if="city === 'portland'" src="/portland.jpg" alt="Portland" />
    <img v-if="city === 'san francisco'" src="/san-francisco.jpg" alt="San Francisco" />
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  props: {
    city: {
      type: String,
      required: true,
    },
  },
};
</script>
```

This code creates a Vue component that will display an image of either Portland or San Francisco, based on the value of the `city` prop.

5. Next, place the images you want to use (`portland.jpg` and `san-francisco.jpg`) inside the `static` directory in your Nuxt.js project. Make sure the file names match the `src` attribute in the `CityImage.vue` component.

6. Now it's time to use the `CityImage.vue` component within a Nuxt.js page. Open the `pages/index.vue` file (or create another page if you prefer) and add the following code:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <CityImage :city="selectedCity" />
    <button @click="selectCity('portland')">Portland</button>
    <button @click="selectCity('san francisco')">San Francisco</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import CityImage from '~/components/CityImage.vue';

export default {
  components: {
    CityImage,
  },
  data() {
    return {
      selectedCity: 'portland',
    };
  },
  methods: {
    selectCity(city) {
      this.selectedCity = city;
    },
  },
};
</script>
```

This code imports the `CityImage.vue` component and uses it with the `selectedCity` data property. It also provides buttons to change the value of `selectedCity`.

7. Start the development server using the following command:

```bash
npm run dev
```

Now, when you visit your Nuxt.js application, you will see the image of either Portland or San Francisco, depending on the value of the `selectedCity` variable, and you can change the value using the buttons provided.
